Subject: [PATCH] Capture independent XNU debug and ramoops live QR streams

Source: pristine XNU ac9718fb1af618d5ce8678d0dc6e8a58f252216f, with every selected
pinned arm64e, device pre-OS, macOS 27.3 and device 0001..0013 patch applied in
order to an independent relevant-file fixture. UniXNU is pinned at
0c1bac3d51a3009dca1708b15a5198f4b43d057a; fetched caches are read-only. The exact
pre-change source SHA-256 values are:
  osfmk/arm/arm_init.c: 936c00f99ed80065526031114384f6937503a5ffd93e671231f75d97aaa99f91
  osfmk/kern/startup.c: c4f226c70e0ae819609e56837298f071c979a22fe8d71986c4c06f8de11bdcc2
  osfmk/kern/Makefile: fb49c1aebe945bf729d1290de5208bcb5a6c7527e9c5dc82c3ad178b8bbe1e6b
  osfmk/console/video_console.c: e6196daa2cb7d9c7ae080f3a75e70f5f63da5e9cea11f7d32bf3f24efb08cea5
  osfmk/console/serial_console.c: 79dc60755a8cdfaa128aaa701679b7dbe6b34e7a5919595a57e5c75c269c62cd
  pexpert/arm/pe_kprintf.c: 747898eb01cd46bf7df6c6c91f4d7f156d86976dace72c19796787297f0e6595

Reason: export independent current-boot XNU debug and real ramoops text while
normal XNU execution continues. The strong LiveLog archive initializes after
ramoops/VM setup, polls one bounded frame after startup messages and before
original startup callbacks, and refreshes after console acquisition/rebinding.
Calls occur after kprintf returns, never under its lock. The archive owns its
rendering/worker lifetime and must not use a shared diagnostic stack or alter
TTBR state. The pre-machine_startup freeze retains up to 512 XNU debug bytes in every
left-stream export, with length-delimited metadata and a current-tail range;
there is no delay, terminal checkpoint or replacement of the original call.

Direct PE_kputc serial_putc_crlf/serial_putc output now reaches both ramoops
and the independent zero-initialized 64 KiB XNU capture ring. The normal
_cnputc path and disabled-console helper mirror to the same pure capture
provider once per non-CR byte. Capture never renders or polls and starts
before display init; display init does not clear the ring. The
serial_putc_options callback remains untapped: _cnputc already mirrors that
path, so adding another tap would duplicate every console character. Only VC
text is suppressed while the actual native LiveLog provider is active; the
ramoops/CDC taps and serial delivery remain intact. When graphics acquisition
sets disableConsoleOutput, _cnputs, console_write and console_write_unbuffered
now retain the same character taps before their existing early return; enabled
paths are unchanged and no UART call is added in the disabled path.
Every new call is guarded
by XNU_TB710FU_LIVE_LOG, and no missing-provider stub is added. No hardware,
reset, storage, security or root acceptance policy changes are made.

Native run 44112832250, job 134142786467, at device commit
eb1f2bc70f08d410dbcb7e182372735d38bb018a failed first in
pexpert/arm/pe_kprintf.c:3:10: kern/tb710fu_ramoops.h was not found.
The exact bootstrap log SHA-256 is
374313355bc425ddd80816cdd0fd35306bf6ca01c5757506536ffaf81539eacd
(line 5859), retained in artifact 14342089034. Its ZIP SHA-256 is
048e5fd75d02a477b729e92c3badf158adec63c02bf80e9d99f9537b7583bb27.
The later corecrypto "No XNU symbol names" is a consequence of the incomplete
bootstrap, not a crypto compiler failure. Export both real device headers via
osfmk/kern/Makefile XNU_ONLY_EXPORTS, beside the official boot_progress.h.
This is the same pinned cross-subproject header mechanism; no declaration
stub, extra include path or official script replacement is introduced.

Verification: all source patches were checked and actually applied in order
in the isolated fixture, reconstructed from pristine XNU at the source commit
above using all 140 selected patches and the failed run's exact ordered input
manifest (the first 139 input hashes are unchanged). The repaired final patch
passes git apply --check and applies, and both real headers occur once in the
XNU_ONLY_EXPORTS list. This is a source integration/patch-application check;
dual-stream sender/capture/display and actual extracted console-function host
checks separately validate range metadata, early backup retention after ring
wrap, stream isolation, bounded alternating frames and exact once capture;
the actual enabled native objects, strong imports, final archive definitions
and strict prelink require the separate supported-macOS CI build. Framebuffer,
ramoops persistence and hardware startup require device evidence.

